# Q57 silence watchdog + Q58 stop/start cycles, real hardware (2026-10-31 23:10-23:20)

## Q57: a hung radio is now detected instead of trusted

`Child` gained `LastOutputUtc` (stamped on every stdout/stderr line);
`Supervisor.SilentSeconds(name)` exposes it; `TrayApp.CheckSilence` runs in the 5 s
watchdog:

  radio alive and silent > 30 s -> log "radio silent for N s (hit X); restarting the
  chain", `Running=false`, `RequestStart(true)`. A second hit within 3 minutes raises
  the balloon "网卡没有响应，已经自动重启过两次。请把网卡拔下来、重新插上。"

Why silence is the right signal: the radio prints a `stats cb=...` line every ~5 s even
with no traffic, so a process stuck inside a USB call is silent while still looking
alive to the process table.

Deliberate-hang test: started the chain, then suspended `airbridge_radio.exe` with
`NtSuspendProcess` at 23:10:47 (exactly the "alive but making no progress" shape).

  23:11:19  "radio silent for 32 s (hit 1); restarting the chain"
  23:11:35  new radio process started (the suspended one cannot answer the stop event,
            so it was killed after the 15 s grace - expected, it is frozen by definition)

Detection and restart work. A frozen process cannot be shut down politely, which is why
the second hit escalates to "replug" instead of looping forever.

## Q58 residual: is the RX thread joined now?

Measured: no. The stop path now calls `radio->Stop()` when the worker is still inside
`StartRxLoop`, waits another 2 s, and the worker STILL does not return, so it is
detached and the process exits immediately:

  "rx loop stopped; waiting for the rx thread"
  "rx thread still inside StartRxLoop; stopping the device"
  "rx thread did not return; detaching"
  "device stopped; releasing radio" / "shutdown: done" -> "stopped (exit 0)"

So the detach is not a leftover of the old code but a property of devourer's RX loop:
neither `StopRxLoop()` nor `Stop()` makes `StartRxLoop` return on this path. The race it
implies is process-exit-only (the detached thread can touch radio/queue while teardown
runs), and repeated cycles have not crashed.

## stop/start cycles with the new ordering (no replug at any point)

  23:15:42 ready (40 s)  ->  23:17:34 "shutdown: done" exit 0
  23:17:40 ready (43 s)  ->  23:19:45 "shutdown: done" exit 0

The dongle stayed usable across both cycles and across the suspended-process kill.
